cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  twincalib: digital-twin calibration with local identifiability analysis,
  profile-likelihood intervals, and a measurement of how much a learned residual
  model absorbs identifiability failure
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-15
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A calibration package for parameterised digital twins that reports what the
  data cannot determine before it reports any parameter value. Local
  identifiability is computed from the singular value decomposition of the
  weighted log-parameter sensitivity matrix: numerical rank, condition number,
  the Brun collinearity index, and the near-null directions reported as the
  parameter combinations that are unconstrained rather than as a warning. Two of
  those combinations are known in closed form and are used as known-answer
  tests: a single-node linear model whose solution depends on three parameters
  only through two ratios, giving an exact null direction of (1,1,1)/sqrt(3)
  recovered to 8.8e-09 per component; and a two-node radiator design under
  constant illumination in which an absorbed solar load and a parasitic load
  enter identically, giving an exact null direction recovered to 1.3e-09 per
  component. A pseudo-inverse covariance reports a 1.9 per cent relative
  standard deviation for a parameter in the second case that the data cannot
  constrain at all; the package replaces that with infinity and reports the
  profile-likelihood interval as open below, while showing that the SUM of the
  two loads is identified to 0.6 per cent. The baseline estimator is weighted
  nonlinear least squares on the physics alone, which attains the Cramer-Rao
  bound to within 0.787 to 1.493 over thirty measured cells, and whose profile
  intervals have a pooled coverage of 0.9306 with exact interval
  [0.8453, 0.9771] against a nominal 0.95. That coverage falls to 0.3750 once a
  declared model-form discrepancy is present, which is reported rather than
  softened. A Gaussian-process residual model is then fitted on top of the
  physics, with a predictive standard deviation, and benchmarked on held-out
  trajectories: it improves prediction by 39 per cent on the sound design and
  makes the physical parameters 35 per cent worse in root-mean-square log error
  on the same experiment, and it makes them worse on three of the four designs
  measured. How much of its improvement absorbs an identifiability failure is
  measured, not asserted, by projecting the learned residual field onto the
  strong, weak and orthogonal subspaces of the physics sensitivity matrix: on
  the structurally non-identifiable design 12.7 per cent of its energy lies in
  directions the data cannot constrain against 0.16 per cent on the sound one,
  the two ranges over six configurations are disjoint, and the ratio between
  them is reported as unstable. All data is synthetic, generated by committed
  scripts from fixed seeds, and no parameter value in the thermal model is a
  measurement of flight hardware. Validation level 3, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - digital-twin
  - identifiability
  - profile-likelihood
  - fisher-information
  - parameter-estimation
  - model-discrepancy
  - spacecraft-thermal
  - aerospace
